<p data-start=3D"4488" data-end=3D"4506" class=3D"">Dans cet exemple :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4507" data-end=3D"4819">
<li data-start=3D"4507" data-end=3D"4574" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4509" data-end=3D"4574" class=3D"">L'interface <code data-=
start=3D"4521" data-end=3D"4536">PaymentMethod</code> d=C3=A9finit une m=C3=
=A9thode <code data-start=3D"4557" data-end=3D"4573">processPayment</code>.=
</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4575" data-end=3D"4663" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4577" data-end=3D"4663" class=3D"">Les classes <code data-=
start=3D"4589" data-end=3D"4601">CreditCard</code> et <code data-start=3D"4=
605" data-end=3D"4613">PayPal</code> impl=C3=A9mentent cette m=C3=A9thode d=
e mani=C3=A8re diff=C3=A9rente.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4664" data-end=3D"4819" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"4666" data-end=3D"4819" class=3D"">La fonction <code data-=
start=3D"4678" data-end=3D"4698">processTransaction</code> peut accepter n'=
importe quelle m=C3=A9thode de paiement, car elle attend un objet qui impl=
=C3=A9mente l'interface <code data-start=3D"4803" data-end=3D"4818">Payment=
Method</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"4821" data-end=3D"4855" class=3D"">2.4 Interface et Polym=
orphisme</h3>
<p data-start=3D"4857" data-end=3D"5040" class=3D"">Une interface permet d'=
impl=C3=A9menter un <strong data-start=3D"4895" data-end=3D"4912">polymorph=
isme</strong>. C'est-=C3=A0-dire que vous pouvez utiliser diff=C3=A9rents t=
ypes d'objets de mani=C3=A8re interchangeable s'ils respectent la m=C3=AAme=
 interface.</p>
<p data-start=3D"5042" data-end=3D"5163" class=3D"">Dans l'exemple pr=C3=A9=
c=C3=A9dent, on a pu traiter <code data-start=3D"5084" data-end=3D"5096">Cr=
editCard</code> et <code data-start=3D"5100" data-end=3D"5108">PayPal</code=
> de mani=C3=A8re polymorphe via l'interface <code data-start=3D"5147" data=
-end=3D"5162">PaymentMethod</code>.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"5165" data-end=3D"5168" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"5170" data-end=3D"5205" class=3D"">3. Les Classes Abstrai=
tes en PHP</h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"5207" data-end=3D"5256" class=3D"">3.1 D=C3=A9finition et=
 R=C3=B4le des Classes Abstraites</h3>
<p data-start=3D"5258" data-end=3D"5582" class=3D"">Une <strong data-start=
=3D"5262" data-end=3D"5282">classe abstraite</strong> est une classe qui ne=
 peut pas =C3=AAtre instanci=C3=A9e directement. Elle sert de base pour d'a=
utres classes qui devront h=C3=A9riter d'elle et impl=C3=A9menter certaines=
 m=C3=A9thodes. Une classe abstraite peut contenir des <strong data-start=
=3D"5484" data-end=3D"5507">m=C3=A9thodes abstraites</strong> (sans impl=C3=
=A9mentation) ou des <strong data-start=3D"5537" data-end=3D"5559">m=C3=A9t=
hodes concr=C3=A8tes</strong> (avec impl=C3=A9mentation).</p>
<p data-start=3D"5584" data-end=3D"5691" class=3D"">Les <strong data-start=
=3D"5588" data-end=3D"5611">m=C3=A9thodes abstraites</strong> doivent =C3=
=AAtre impl=C3=A9ment=C3=A9es dans les classes qui h=C3=A9ritent de la clas=
se abstraite.</p>
